# Burla Architecture

Burla is a distributed compute system with one user-facing function: `remote_parallel_map(function, inputs)`. It runs a Python function across many VMs in the user's cloud account. Self-hosted; runs on GCP or AWS (identical behavior, selected by the `CLOUD_PROVIDER` env var on the head).

## The four services

| Service | Runtime | Purpose |
|---------|---------|---------|
| `client/` (pypi: `burla`) | User's laptop / notebook | Entry point (`remote_parallel_map`). Pickles UDF, picks nodes, uploads inputs, streams results. Ships a vendored copy of `main_service` so the head can run client-hosted. |
| `main_service/` (the "head") | Default: inside the `burla` pip package on the user's machine (`IN_CLIENT_HOSTED_MODE`, started by rpm / `burla dashboard`, see `client/src/burla/_local_head.py`). Optional via `burla deploy`: one small always-on VM (GCE or EC2). | Holds authoritative live cluster state in memory, persists history to SQLite, boots/deletes node VMs, hosts the React dashboard, handles auth. |
| `node_service/` | Runs on each node VM | Per-VM orchestrator. Owns input/results queues, spawns workers, runs job watcher. Nodes carry no cloud credentials unless the shared filesystem is enabled, except client-hosted Azure nodes receive a short-lived delete lease. They remove themselves when idle or orphaned. |
| `worker_service/` (the file `node_service/src/node_service/worker_server.py`) | Inside user's Docker container, one per CPU core | Executes user code. Talks to `node_service` over a raw TCP socket, not HTTP. |

There is no separate `worker_service/` top-level directory: the worker script lives next to `node.py` in the node_service and is mounted into each container at runtime.

## Data flow

- Client → `main_service` (HTTP): start job (node selection + grow), job signals, auth.
- Client → `node_service` (HTTP): upload UDF + inputs, poll results, direct heartbeats.
- Node → `main_service` (HTTP): state push every ~1s (`PUT /v1/nodes/{id}/state`); the response carries `host` and the job signal set back down (cancel, `all_inputs_uploaded`, `client_has_all_results`, quorum info). Logs batch-POST to the head.
- `main_service` → VMs: boot/delete instances via a compute-provider interface (`main_service/src/main_service/providers/`, GCE + EC2 implementations).
- `node_service` → `worker_server.py` (TCP bytes): run UDF.

Inputs also move node-to-node mid-job via `/jobs/{job_id}/get_inputs` + `/jobs/{job_id}/ack_transfer` (pull-based work stealing): the peer list comes from the head's `/v1/jobs/{id}/peers`. Cloud nodes route head and peer traffic through the relay, so jobs can span regions without inbound firewall rules or private-network peering.

## State / coordination

There is no database in the live path. Coordination state is head-centric:

- Live state (nodes + jobs) lives in [main_service/src/main_service/cluster_state.py](../../main_service/src/main_service/cluster_state.py): plain dicts, mutated by node pushes and endpoints, with in-memory pub/sub feeding the dashboard SSE streams.
- History (job records, job/node logs, usage, cluster_config) lives in SQLite via [main_service/src/main_service/history.py](../../main_service/src/main_service/history.py) at `/var/lib/burla/history.db`. Written on transitions; never read in the live path. The head reloads active nodes + running jobs from it at startup.
- In-memory node state lives in the `SELF` dict in [node_service/src/node_service/__init__.py](../../node_service/src/node_service/__init__.py): reset by `REINIT_SELF`. `SELF["reported_status"]` is what the push loop reports to the head.
- The head is a stateful singleton: exactly one always-on instance per cluster.

## Dev modes

Set via `IN_LOCAL_DEV_MODE` env var. Both dev modes run `main_service` on the
developer's own machine against the working tree, and both are namespaced per
checkout (`BURLA_CLUSTER_NAME`, defaulting to the worktree name) so many run at
once on one machine. Dev happens in AWS; GCP is production-only. See the
`burla-parallel-dev` skill.

- **local-dev-mode**: each node is a privileged Docker container acting as a fake VM: it runs its own docker daemon (`node_service/local_dev_entrypoint.sh`), so workers are inner containers owned by that node exactly like on a real VM and `node_service`'s worker code has no local-dev branches. The head runs as a host subprocess straight from the checkout (same client-hosted path as remote-dev, via `python -m burla._local_dev`), so there is no head image. `make local-dev`. Uses `LOCAL_DEV_CONFIG` in `main_service/__init__.py` (1 node by default, `LOCAL_DEV_NODE_QUANTITY` to raise it). Nodes reach the head at `host.docker.internal` (resolved to an IPv4 by the entrypoint so inner workers can reach it too); the head reaches nodes on their published `127.0.0.1` ports. The default worker image is `docker load`ed from `_image_seed/` at node boot so inner daemons don't re-download it. Needs a working AWS identity and a saved cluster token (nodes authorize callers against the backend's user list for this cluster id), so `make local-dev` fails fast telling you to `aws sso login` / `burla login`. The head port, docker network, container labels, and node host ports are all namespaced per checkout; workers can't collide across clusters at all since they live inside their node.
- **remote-dev-mode**: `main_service` runs as a local subprocess (the client-hosted path in [client/src/burla/_local_head.py](../../client/src/burla/_local_head.py), started by `make remote-dev`), node VMs are real EC2 instances in the Burla test AWS account. Each head mints its own relay subdomain, and nodes reach it through the relay. Nodes run the checkout's current git branch, so node/worker changes must be pushed.
- **prod**: `main_service` on a head VM that runs the released `burla` wheel in a stock `python` container (GCE cos + startup script / EC2 + docker; the container `pip install burla==<version>` then runs `uvicorn main_service:app`, so there is no prebuilt head image), nodes on Compute Engine or EC2.

## Version compatibility

`CURRENT_BURLA_VERSION` and `MIN_COMPATIBLE_CLIENT_VERSION` are defined in [main_service/src/main_service/__init__.py](../../main_service/src/main_service/__init__.py). Client version is checked when jobs start; incompatible clients get `VersionMismatch`.

